<p>You know, that's a good point… Allow me to clarify. What I don't like is that he is, in fact, essentially uncatchable, and can go anywhere he wants and do anything he wants. Even though it's not played up as such in the article, it still comes as sue-ish to me- That is, it breeches the line between "the scp is so cool and I want to do that" and "oh that poor poor man" in a way that's distracting. Granted, messing with that <em>could</em> mess up the total isolation felt by 451, but I think it would also introduce a necessary "still just a human" quality. I could, of course, be wrong, but this is what I imagine.</p>

